Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:05.829 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  my earliest childhood memories  was playing with legos  i loved  building lego cities with ships for the  pirates  and hospitals for the doctors  and trucks for the firefighters  it was my world of endless possibilities  and i was the architect of my own  dreams  i can also remember  that whenever i finished building a city  i would always have  one piece left over  and it was always  the same piece  it was always  the woman  only the woman  does not have a uniform  she did not have a function  she did not have a role  she was not a chef  she was not a builder she was  simply  a woman  so i didn't know what to do with her and  i'd  look around my city try to see where she  fit maybe stick her inside a house and  hope no one would notice  why was the woman  the only character that looked like me  an afterthought in my own dream  i can trace back to this memory my  decision to become an entrepreneur and a  designer because i wanted to play by my  own rules and design gave me the skills  and the tools  to build a world of my own  and as a designer i know  that the world around us is shaped by  design choices  so for that simple reason  if we have designed women out  we can design  woman in  how  the first step to jump start this change  is to realize that the world around us  is the result of what researchers call  the generic masculine  the generic masculine means that we  you  and i  we interpret everything as male unless  specified as female  it means  that when we say human  we mean man  and by making man our normal standard  human  we erase  woman from the picture  and her absences  are everywhere  there is a history right  in the policy we pursue in the cities we  plan  and the algorithms we code and in the  products we design  her absences can take the shape  of an unthreatening toy  but they can also  be deadly  women are 17  more likely to die in a car crash than  men  according to the u.s department of  transportation  why  because cars are designed for men's  bodies  car crash test dummies are designed  after men's measurements  and car seat belts are designed for  men's torsos so they simply do not  account for breast tissue or pregnancy  there are more fetal deaths from car  crashes than any other trauma and i ask  why is it this way  the leading cause of death of women in  the usa  is cardiovascular disease yet only 25  of hospital emergency treatments are  given to women  why  because diagnosis criteria were designed  to fit men's symptoms and unfortunately  a woman's heart attack looks nothing  like a man's  instead of experiencing chest and arm  pain women often experience  breathlessness  stomach pain  nausea and fatigue  her symptoms are referred to as a  typical  let that word sink in  a  typical  so instead of receiving  the treatment that she needs and  deserves  she is sent home with a prescription for  anxiety  why is it this way  and even when bias design does not kill  women it makes their life so much harder  unsafe and tiring  when we take men's perceptions patterns  and behaviors  and make them our golden rule  when we assume that what he wants does  and needs  represents us all  then our design is flawed from the  get-go  and once you see it you cannot unsee it  because it is everywhere around us  and since we stand on the stanford  campus  let's glance  at our surroundings  i see a university campus with outdoor  lighting so dim  and dispersed that female students who  live here feel unsafe at night and think  twice before choosing their paths  why didn't stanford make their walkways  brighter  because  lighting was not designed with woman in  mind  the very lengthy and very detailed  stanford lighting guidelines make it  clear light is meant to orient travelers  and to unify campus architecture  and places no emphasis on safety or  security perceptions that are central  for their female community  i can also see a full-time executive  program  that is only 25  female  why can't stanford get more executive  women to apply and come once admitted  what invisible hurdles are still there  since this program targets men and women  over 30 i'd recommend we take a long  hard look  at child care services provided  because half of the men in our program  are fathers and only three women are  mothers  how do the systems in place fail to  account for the unique challenges faced  by mothers that father surpass mothers  seven to one  why is it  this way  i believe we're in the right place and  in the right time  to redesign a world that also works for  women  because we are no longer the children  playing with the lego sets we were given  i invite you all  to reframe the way that you think of  your role from decision makers in  business in education and in policy  and to think of yourself  as designers  to everyone listening hear this  you  are a designer  because whenever you're making a  decision about what business model to  build what policy to pursue  or what strategy to roll out you are  making design choices  dear designers  know that you are powerful  use your power to advocate  for your user and their needs  all of your users  and all of their needs because they are  not in the room when you're making a  decision about them so be their voice  this responsibility  lies on all of us not just on women  and it influences all products all  services and all policies  and i ask you to go beyond the business  school questions why this  and why now  and ask the most important design  question  why is it  this way  leaders of today and leaders of tomorrow  how will you  design a world  that also works  for women  [Applause]  [Music]  you

